 at which the program stops by itself. Slices 6d-a (carrier, reference lane), 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry) and 6e (backends) land it.\n35: +\n36:  ### 0.2 The answer\n37:  \n38: -A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\n39: -machine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\n40: -frames, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n41: -(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\n42: +A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, which is a `run` state `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` at any instruction boundary (§1.3) or one of its two delivery states, `act` or `tail`, with the saved window, the delivery record and the pending wait the evaluator mints when it parks (frozen §2.4, §4.2); and a task is the product of its activation, its ordered waits and its ordered completed wakes (§2.6); plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\n43:  environment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\n44:  A and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\n45:  evaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\n46:  profile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\n47:  window as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n48:  `[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n49: -`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n50: +`:parked-table` rows, the `:run`, `:ready` and `:completion` rows (6d) replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n51:  `free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\n52:  validator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\n53:  code and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\n54: @@ -175,11 +176,12 @@ and 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n55:  **DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n56:  `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n57:  `entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\n58: -and the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n59: +and the park). Three closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n60:  \n61:  ```clojure\n62:  act  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\n63:  tail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n64: +run  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K}      ; between instructions; no deliver, no pending\n65:  ret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n66:  ```\n67:  \n68: @@ -239,6 +241,22 @@ two are in bijection (§2.6).\n69:  \n70:  ---\n71:  \n72: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n73: +\n74: +```clojure\n75: +;; run state (6d)\n76: +{:yin.k/state :run\n77: + :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n78: + :yin.k/env {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n79: + :yin.k/window {r V …} ; exactly L(n), retaining nil-valued keys\n80: + :yin.k/k […]}        ; no deliver, no pending\n81: +```\n82: +\n83: +### 1.3 States between instructions, and the task product\n84: +\n85: +The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks by itself. They are special cases of its general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect; atomicity of a step is not transactionality of the outside world (an append performed before a host exception is not undone). A task is a **product**: at most one active activation (the `run`), its ordered pending waits, its ordered completed wakes (the ready queue: a woken wait with the value, status and cursor update its effect produced, carried by the `:ready` row of §2.3), its parked table, and its stores. The scheduler constructs every combination: resuming one of several woken waits leaves the others queued and the unresolved ones pending (`engine.cljc:2294-2312`, `:2305`). Every such state is **representable**. The lift refuses only what is not **portable** (a value with no wire form), not **satisfied** (code or dependencies the receiver lacks), or a **hold** (a driver-internal observation in flight: §7.1 step 1), never a pc. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc of a body: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`), and the stack kernel's operand region at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)` above a dead prefix (§4.3); both are checks (§5.6), not theorems. A root whose task has an active activation has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\n86: +\n87: +\n88:  ## 2. The row carrier\n89:  \n90:  The row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n91: @@ -293,6 +311,9 @@ and `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n92:  | Kind | Slots | Notes |\n93:  |---|---|---|\n94:  | `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n95: +| `:run` | `segment-A pc-N env-cref window-cref k-cref` | between instructions; no delivery, no pending |\n96: +| `:ready` | `state-cref completion-cref value optional-status updates-cref optional-refusal` | an ordered completed outcome |\n97: +| `:completion` | discriminated by the table below | completed operations, separate from outstanding pendings |\n98:  | `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n99:  | `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n100:  | `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n101: @@ -309,6 +330,25 @@ must be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\n102:  redundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n103:  `pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n104:  \n105: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n106: +\n107: +A ready row contains a delivery-state reference, completed-operation descriptor, outcome value, optional status, cursor-update row and optional raised-refusal value. Its delivery state is act or tail, never run. It is judged in context `:ready`, without an outstanding pending.\n108: +\n109: +Completed descriptors cover ordinary reads and writes, FFI response and request-append outcomes, link-request and link-response outcomes, and install outcomes. Link/install completion does not require the request transport or install entry that existed before completion. A successful link/install outcome may have no status. Try failures return their carried value; ordinary require failures restore `:link-refused` and refusal data for the terminal-resume path.\n110: +\n111: +Slot 9 is the sole cell census. A ready update pairs an existing canonical C with its independently emitted cell body. One C may name different bodies across the census and ordered update rows. Restore maps C through the census’s fresh-key map and applies the update body carried by that ready row.\n112: +\n113: +| Completion tag | Slots after the tag | Restored engine meaning |\n114: +|---|---|---|\n115: +| `:next` | `C` | Ordinary completed reader; reconstruct cursor-ref and stream-id through the census. |\n116: +| `:put` | `stream-cref, datom-V, optional-op-id-D` | Ordinary completed append outcome; never append again. |\n117: +| `:ffi` | `C, call-id-D, op-D` | Completed FFI response reader; restore engine reason `:next` and correlation fields. |\n118: +| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D` | Completed request append; restore `:request-sent true`, so successful resume creates the response wait rather than delivering the append result to the guest. |\n119: +| `:link-request` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D` | Completed link-request outcome; no outstanding request or response transport is required. |\n120: +| `:link-response` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D` | Completed link-response outcome. |\n121: +| `:install` | `module-S, try?-D` | Completed initializer outcome; no remaining install entry is required. |\n122: +\n123: +\n124:  ### 2.4 Pending rows\n125:  \n126:  **DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n127: @@ -377,7 +417,7 @@ markers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n128:  [T :task\n129:     version          ; 3\n130:     contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n131: -   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n132: +   kind             ; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted\n133:     role             ; :root | :child\n134:     id-counter       ; N\n135:     free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n136: @@ -388,10 +428,12 @@ markers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n137:     requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n138:     code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n139:     installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n140: -   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n141: +   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:running, :blocked, :parked)\n142:     parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n143:     result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n144: -   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's t