pleted-outcome status/refusal checks and shape-only IDs at slot 18; then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, response `:yin.link/id` equals non-nil parent, manifest-named derivation record hashes correctly and connects the manifest tree to the image A, child kind `:blocked` at phase `:parked`, `:running` at phase `:running`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-link-id`, `:install-image`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n439:  | dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n440: -| custody | `check-custody!` | `custody-baseline` over the tree; fork refused under `:exclusive`; halted exclusive needs no grant; blocked/parked exclusive validates grant evidence, pack checkpoint, binding, lease/holder, arbitration, epoch, live tenure and input prefix, then protection in both directions and op-id presence against enrollment; `:fenced` skips grant/protection | grammar's `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`, `:yin.k/not-holder`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` |\n441: +| custody | `check-custody!` | `custody-baseline` over the tree; fork refused under `:exclusive`; halted exclusive needs no grant; running/blocked/parked exclusive validates grant evidence, pack checkpoint, binding, lease/holder, arbitration, epoch, live tenure and input prefix, then protection in both directions and op-id presence against enrollment; `:fenced` skips grant/protection | grammar's `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`, `:yin.k/not-holder`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` |\n442:  | modules | `check-modules!` | Whole-tree module address and used host-export profile equality; carried install children require receiver admission; after custody, before attach | `:yin.k/unsatisfied :module-pin-mismatch`, `:host-export-mismatch`, or collected missing with `:yin.k/discovery :complete` |\n443:  \n444:  **Amended 2026-10-16 (module-pins, R4).** After custody validation and\n445: @@ -1848,7 +1953,7 @@ profiles refuse terminally as `:module-pin-mismatch` or `:host-export-mismatch`;\n446:  absent bindings, absent exports and unadmitted install children are collected\n447:  in one `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/discovery :complete` and\n448:  `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules … :yin.k/host-exports …}` (only nonempty keys).\n449: -The receiver's registry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n450: +The receiver's registry pins and slices are kept as given. Its task-local lowered bindings are recreated through `engine/receive-module` after the carried images and stores, so they name the restored task's code coordinates and carried live stores win. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n451:  :kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n452:  :install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\n453:  census are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\n454: @@ -1869,6 +1974,80 @@ A grant's `:checkpoint` names the pack address, never the root id.\n455:  \n456:  ---\n457:  \n458: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n459: +\n460: +A running task restores:\n461: +\n462: +```clojure\n463: +{:control {:segment local :pc pc}\n464: + :window W\n465: + :env E\n466: + :k (when (seq K) K)\n467: + :halted? false\n468: + :blocked? false\n469: + :value nil\n470: + :wait-set entries}\n471: +```\n472: +\n473: +Restore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n474: +\n475: +Every kind restores slot 18 in order. Resolve the ready state and completion descriptor, reconstruct the engine entry described in §3.1, then restore its value, status, refusal and resource updates. Update keys map through the slot-9 C→fresh-resource-key map; update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9.\n476: +\n477: +No completed operation is polled or reissued by lower. A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition. Link/install completion restores without resurrecting retired pending resources.\n478: +\n479: +A fenced restore retains ready entries in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`, alongside recovery waits and parked records, and exposes an empty scheduler queue. Running control remains available for byte-identical relift, protected by the 6d-a closed-gate guard.\n480: +\n481: +\n482: +### 7.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n483: +\n484: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n485: +\n486: +An exclusive running, blocked or parked root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only. A completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write. Completed outcomes never acquire publication or execution authority from their history.\n487: +\n488: +Stage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n489: +\n490: +Progress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it. Stage-local holder transitions, tenure cuts and durable export are implemented and tested by 6d-b. The 6d-a carrier admits running custody subjects and retains fenced recovery data.\n491: +\n492: +Direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` refuse `:exporting` and `:ended` before guest, scheduler or bridge work. The guard is outside the instruction loop. A fenced running restore preserves control, window, environment, K and ordered completed outcomes for byte-identical relift; nothing runs during lower.\n493: +\n494: +### 7.4 Fuel\n495: +\n496: +**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, with positive exact integer `n`.\n497: +\n498: +One invocation performs:\n499: +\n500: +1. Closed-gate check.\n501: +2. Exactly one raw budgeted action:\n502: +   - If control is present, `vm-hot` with fuel `n`.\n503: +   - Otherwise, one budgeted scheduler round.\n504: +3. If the result is blocked and an explicit bridge is installed, at most one `ffi/bridge-step`.\n505: +4. Return that resulting machine immediately.\n506: +\n507: +The implementation does **not** wrap the raw action in unchanged `ffi/maybe-run`. A handled response or malformed-request diagnostic does not restart the runner or bridge inside the same invocation. The next caller invocation resumes progress.\n508: +\n509: +The bounded bridge helper may be private to the semantic runner or a bounded variant beside `ffi/maybe-run`; its contract is the four steps above. Existing unrestricted `vm/run` bridge behavior remains unchanged for live machines.\n510: +\n511: +**Budgeted scheduler round.**\n512: +\n513: +Propagate the budget through `check-wait-set`, `poll-links`, `advance-installs` and `advance-install`. Each visited child is invoked once through `run-fuel child n`; nested children use the same rule. Preserve existing gate stamping and the prohibition on running children under closed gates.\n514: +\n515: +After the sweep, resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation. A running child retains phase `:running`; blocked children retain `:parked`; halted children complete installation as today.\n516: +\n517: +The dispatch tests **control presence**, not merely `engine/active-continuation?`: the latter only tests blocked/halted flags and can classify a control-less ingress-ready machine as active. Therefore no `run-fuel` path falls through `vm-hot` into its unbudgeted scheduler fallback. A quiescent control-less machine takes the budgeted no-work round and returns.\n518: +\n519: +**Per-tree bound.**\n520: +\n521: +Each task visited during the invocation receives at most one budgeted action and at most one bridge step. Each task executes at most `n` guest instructions. For `t` distinct tasks visited during the invocation, including children created during the round, the instruction bound is `n × t`. No task is revisited with a fresh budget inside that invocation.\n522: +\n523: +**The honest guarantee**, copied verbatim into the documents:\n524: +\n525: +> Fuel counts completed guest instructions. One `run-fuel` invocation executes at most `n` instructions per visited task, at most one scheduler round per visited inactive task, and at most one bridge step per visited task. A bridge result never restarts execution inside that invocation. No wall-clock bound is claimed for an instruction, host function, stream constructor, variable resolution, bridge step or scheduler sweep. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness and the composition chooses `n`.\n526: +\n527: +`vm/step` keeps its telemetry behavior. `run-fuel` adds no per-fuel snapshot; existing park, resume and bridge telemetry remains.\n528: +\n529: +Running install children use the same carrier and receiver-admission checks as other tasks. The backends continue to refuse install children.\n530: +\n531: +\n532:  ## 8. Slices, gates, entry conditions\n533:  \n534:  Verification follows `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: G0 per iteration on\n535: @@ -2248,6 +2427,43 @@ design names no kernel edit beyond 6c-pre (**Amended 2026-10-13\n536:  \n537:  ---\n538:  \n539: +**Dead ambient operands (static domain).** The non-tail set equation holds where the enclosing application's pending reads are still reachable. At the operand PCs of an application whose evaluation an operand-position tail call or `:resume` abandons (the corpus's `:resume-operand` and `:tail-operand` fixtures), ambient layout entries are dead per L while remaining in the region: the strongest static truth there is the inclusion `uses(p) ⊆ L(p) ⊆ layout(p) ∪ uses(p)`; the equality is permanently excluded at those pcs. 6e's dead-prefix rule governs the region after the transfer, and 6e's ordered dynamic checks — not the static set equality — carry those pcs.\n540: +\n541: +## 8.6 Slice 6d-a: reference lane\n542: +\n543: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n544: +\n545: +**Acceptance.**\n546: +\n547: +1. **Frame rules.** Run states at worked-example boundaries validate, including body start. Pin PC kind/bounds, window scope, missing/extra keys, forbidden deliver/pending and wrong-context diagnostics. C3 failures on act/return frames are `:pc-body`. Delivery frames use `saved(pc, rd)`; running frames use `L(pc)`.\n548: +\n549: +2. **Row grammar and completed validation.** Round-trip run frames; reject extra slots and incorrect reference kinds. Ready states admit act/tail only. Status is nil or keyword. Pin invalid completion tags, site mismatch, nonboolean link/install `try?`, inconsistent refusal slots, missing update-cell membership, duplicate update keys and malformed completed operation IDs. These lower refusals occur before attachment.\n550: +\n551: +3. **Task-product kinds.** Retain running-only, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, blocked-plus-ready and halted-plus-ready cases. Add halted-plus-outstanding-wait with empty queue: pin the existing blocked normalization and dropped result separately. Retain invalid run counts, illegal result/parked-id and empty-blocked cases.\n552: +\n553: +4. **Completed link/install outcomes.** Exercise success, try failure and ordinary raised failure for link/install producers. Capture completed install outcomes after removal of the install entry. Include active control beside a remaining completion. Immediate relift is equal; continuation delivers or raises once, without reconstructing retired pending resources.\n554: +\n555: +5. **Shared cursor.** Two waiters share one cursor. Snapshot after the sweep before any resume. Preserve one canonical cell identity, the slot-9 current body, both values and update rows in order. Assert immediate relift equality and source/receiver continuation equality. Add an encoding case where one C pairs with different bodies across census/update rows.\n556: +\n557: +6. **Completed write and terminal outcomes.** Fork and exclusive completed-write histories round-trip without enrollment or reappend. A malformed present ID refuses at the named slot-18 check. Nil IDs remain admissible where the ungated source produced none. Add a terminal-status ready case whose receiver raises identically, plus the FFI sent-request transition that creates a response wait without a second request append.\n558: +\n559: +7. **Every returned boundary, slow tier.** Run the existing corpus through `run-fuel 1`; lift, lower into isolated worlds, immediately relift, validate frames and continue to the reference outcome. Pin measured counts and the driver coverage classes of §3.6. Constructed post-sweep cases are reported separately. Row 20's setup batch (`s/def!` store definitions executed at runtime) lifts only from the first boundary at which its definitions are answered in the store; the leading boundary is excluded as a **pinned conservative-discharge false refusal of the owner-ratified Q6 family (default 5: keep edge-reachability, derive don't persist)** — recorded per case with its obligations and zero serves, never skipped silently: the driver asserts the excluded boundaries form a contiguous leading run and that every boundary after the first successful lift lifts.\n560: +\n561: +8. **Quick sample and products.** Retain v2’s structural sample and every-boundary small rows. Run the scheduler products and shared-cursor seam through the harness. Retain cursor-ref, reified-continuation, nil-live-register, `ghost` and hold-refusal cases.\n562: +\n563: +9. **Layout gates.** At every reachable corpus PC, check register injectivity and jump/terminator layout availability; at every reachable tail-call PC check `set(uses(p)) = L(p)`; at every other reachable PC check the inclusion `set(uses(p)) ⊆ L(p) ⊆ set(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p))`, and additionally the equality `set(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p)) = L(p)` everywhere **except the pinned dead-ambient operand PCs — exactly `{(:resume-operand 1), (:resume-operand 2), (:tail-operand 1), (:tail-operand 2)}`** — where an operand-position `:resume` (abortive) or tail call (the enclosing application's call is unreachable) makes the enclosing application's pushed operands dead per L while they remain in the static layout and on the stack. At the pinned PCs assert the exclusion's cause: the layout entries outside `L(p)` are exactly those dead ambient operands. The exclusion set is enumerated, never predicate-based: a future corpus entry with a dead ambient layout fails the equality loudly rather than being skipped. On executed paths compare ordered region length and values to the ordered register sequence, including repeated-register agreement. Do not compare that sequence length to `|L(pc)|`. No dead-prefix lift, `slice-window` widening, or running-backend lift is implemented in 6d-a; those remain 6e item 6's scope.\n564: +\n565: +10. **Budgeted children.** Preempt long and nested initializers; lift/lower running children under receiver admission; continue to linked. Count actual instruction transitions and assert at most `n` per visited task and `n × t` overall. No child receives a second budget inside one invocation.\n566: +\n567: +11. **Bounded bridge execution.** A program repeatedly making bridge-served FFI calls returns from every `run-fuel` invocation. Each invocation executes at most one raw action and one bridge step. Repeated invocations reach the reference result. Diagnostic-only bridge input also returns after one step. Pin control-less ingress-ready dispatch to the budgeted round. For a pure active computation lasting at least `n` instructions, compare with `n` single-instruction steps; an earlier park/halt ends the comparison there.\n568: +\n569: +12. **Fencing.** Lower a running snapshot with `{:fenced true}` and exercise direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel`. Each refuses the closed gate before guest, scheduler or bridge work. Assert unchanged control/window/environment/K, unchanged ready recovery data and unchanged relift bytes. Cover `:ended` as well as `:exporting`.\n570: +\n571: +13. **Ready-value portability.** The only unsupported primitive is nested in the ready outcome; register-target lower refuses before attachment. Retain the corresponding portable positive case. Test ready delivery state, outcome and restored descriptor roots independently.\n572: +\n573: +14. **Landing checks.** Kondo clean; the quick tier passes on JVM, Node and Dart with every touched namespace present in logs; the slow tier runs once at landing on all three hosts. Remint fixtures from the integrated grammar. Review stops on satisfied gates, not a fixed round count.\n574: +\n575: +\n576:  ## 9. Verification honesty\n577:  \n578:  - **Hand-derived numbers.** `saved(4, 2) = {1}` and `L(5)` on the worked\n579: @@ -2320,8 +2536,14 @@ design names no kernel edit beyond 6c-pre (**Amended 2026-10-13\n580:  \n581:  ---\n582:  \n583: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n584: +\n585: +**Claims that are checks**: `L(p)` is sufficient at every boundary (law 1 at every step); injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc (item 3); the ordered stack equation at every pc (item 5); the dead-prefix rule (law 1 after a tail transition); the refusal oracle equality (6e). None is a theorem about all programs; each is a gate on the corpus.\n586: +\n587:  ## 10. Findings and proposed amendments\n588:  \n589: +**Historical amendment record.** The phase-specific findings below retain their original text. The any-PC rules in §§1–7 and §8.6 supersede earlier exhaustive state, context and eligibility lists.\n590: +\n591:  Reviewer rulings (codex gpt-6.1-sol, 2026-10-10): F1, F4, U2, U4, U5,\n592:  R1, R2, R7, C1 **ACCEPT**; F2, F3, U1, U3, U6, R3, R4, R5, R6, R8, R9\n593:  **ACCEPT WITH CHANGES**, applied below; dropping `:yin.k/engine`\n594: @@ -2868,3 +3090,7 @@ numbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n595:     integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n596:     (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n597:  10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane.\n598: +\n599: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n600: +\n601: +**Q6 ruling.** Keep reachable by control-flow edges, never by value. At the pc before a constant-dead branch, `ghost` refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with obligations `#{ghost}`. This is the conservative discharge rule, not constant propagation.\n602: diff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\n603: index 0d4f8d22..3a3a9aa2 100644\n604: --- a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\n605: +++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\n606: @@ -656,8 +656,7 @@ of a vector→datoms projection is retired, not deferred.\n607:  \n608:  ### 4.1 The safepoint table (UCF §7.4.1, rewritten)\n609:  \n610: -A safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic\n611: -kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n612: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** The machine's task state between any two instructions, `⟨seg, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` beside its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical and lifts (continuation design §1.3): preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm` (owner, 2026-10-17). A **safepoint** is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The table below enumerates those parking transitions; it is not the set of liftable pcs, which is every pc of every body. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n613:  \n614:  Every wait is one of the two closed states of §2.4 (`act` with\n615:  `{:deliver :rd}` or `tail` with `{:deliver :return}`); an effectful\n616: @@ -684,9 +683,7 @@ parked record and a body of kind `:parked` names it by id (UCF §7.4.3\n617:  r5, \"explicit park is the no-wait shape\"). A **reified continuation**\n618:  (`:current-continuation`) is the one thing here that is *not* a\n619:  safepoint: it is a captured value, never a task state.\n620: -Whole-task quiescence (empty ready queue, UCF §7.4.1) and the existing\n621: -refusal conditions (unsupported observation states, pending close) are\n622: -unchanged.\n623: +A non-empty ready queue is carried as ordered completed wakes (continuation design §2.3); the holds (observation in flight, pending close) remain refusals of the lift as driver seams mid-step.\n624:  \n625:  The \"effectful call, tail\" row is the one place the rewrite is more\n626:  than notation: in the stack machine a tail effect's resume value landed\n627: @@ -760,6 +757,19 @@ the task's initial free environment (continuation design §2, §6.3).\n628:  That amendment is applied to the carrier draft with this design's\n629:  phase 6 (§10; `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §10.3).\n630:  \n631: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n632: +\n633: +```clojure\n634: +;; run state (6d)\n635: +{:yin.k/state :run\n636: + :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n637: + :yin.k/env {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n638: + :yin.k/window {r V …} ; exactly L(n), retaining nil-valued keys\n639: + :yin.k/k […]}        ; no deliver, no pending\n640: +```\n641: +\n642: +The running row is `:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`. A completed outcome is `:ready [state-cref completion-cref value optional-status updates-cref optional-refusal]`; its act/tail delivery state has no outstanding pending. Completion descriptors and cursor updates follow the continuation design’s row grammar.\n643: +\n644:  ### 4.3 Foreign engines (UCF §7.4.2)\n645:  \n646:  A foreign engine's static safepoint map keeps `:yin.safepoint/segment`,\n647: @@ -789,6 +799,12 @@ frames become `E` through the owner chain and `bind-params`, with the\n648:  task's free environment carried explicitly. The AST walker is not a\n649:  frame reconstructor.\n650:  \n651: +**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n652: +\n653: +and for a `run` state at pc `p`, produce the segment `A`, `p`, every register of `L(p)`, `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, and no delivery record or pending; a physical location outside `L(p)`'s image is never read, and an engine that cannot hold a value of `L(p)`, or of any carried frame's window or binding, refuses before attachment (the register kernel's `:continuation-registers`). `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames of all three states; a stack image's `:push` pc relabels to its successor.\n654: +\n655: +Receiver refusal checks include values waiting in completed outcomes, including nested values that become live only on resume.\n656: +\n657:  ## 5. Scheduler, park, resume, FFI, modules\n658:  \n659:  Unchanged in meaning (the deleted v3 design's §3.4, §3.5; UCF §7.6.3):\n660: diff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md\n661: index 82f0bb59..d961b3b7 100644\n662: --- a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md\n663: +++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md\n664: @@ -828,3 +828,5 @@ rows-test entries are task-row roots; ledger entries are pack-byte addresses):\n665:  | ledger checkpoint 4 | `:segment/blake3-ab97dc5502245c63ab49b45088b88989fa143616cadf6e298b8ae90d9694d711` | `:segment/blake3-42b4357517e46880bcf804e32657f483088d0b5821b36249cdd38aa110afb79e` |\n666:  \n667:  Ledger projection digest: `2b26e75e8969428761c4f7813d1ffc886d30d160f2f3e8f80076bbe24ef1f227` → `419e3aa652d79cab6921cf7a6c02972bcfe2d3b88ba472bc03ac8f1a935f27d2`.\n668: +\n669: +**I-9 — 2026-10-16, any-PC.** The current lift rejects active control and queued completed outcomes, while the holder runs unrestricted computation. Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the carrier to run states and complete scheduler products. 6d-a includes completed stream/FFI/link/install outcomes, cursor-update encoding, bounded bridge-aware fuel and closed-gate execution guards. 6d-b adds explicit state retention on tenure failure and holder migration from fuel boundaries. 6e extends backend layouts and transfer laws. Earlier static-safepoint-only lift prescriptions are historical. No compatibility promise is added.\n670: diff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n671: index 9ed6832d..a55f621a 100644\n672: --- a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n673: +++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n674: @@ -398,10 +398,7 @@ response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` whose\n675:  `:yin.module/name` is the row's module and whose `:yin.module/exports`\n676:  is a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map whose `:value` the\n677:  receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`); and the carried\n678: -child task's kind is `:blocked` (`:install-child`), the only kind the\n679: -engine retains at a safepoint (a halted or parked child is linked in the\n680: -sweep it halts in, so phase `:running` is a wire phase the codec accepts\n681: -but the engine never exposes at a safepoint). Lower also requires\n682: +child task's kind is `:blocked` with wire phase `:parked`, or `:running` with wire phase `:running` (`:install-child`). Lower also requires\n683:  non-nil `parent` equal to response `:yin.link/id` (`:install-link-id`).\n684:  The response's `:derivation` record must hash to the manifest's\n685:  `[:yin.module/derivations :yin.semantic/code]` address; its\n686: @@ -430,7 +427,7 @@ No new resolver or independent claimed-image-address check is added.\n687:  [T :task\n688:     version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n689:     contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}, the one profile map (§2)\n690: -   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n691: +   kind             ; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted\n692:     role             ; :root | :child\n693:     id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n694:     free-env-cref    ; a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (continuation §6.3)\n695: @@ -441,10 +438,12 @@ No new resolver or independent claimed-image-address check is added.\n696:     requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (continuation §6.1)\n697:     code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n698:     installs       