eue` and then re-invoke the runner `#(vm-hot* % nil)`. If the machine becomes gated mid-run... actually gates are stamped, and `run-loop` probably stops. Hmm, pre-existing behavior: before this diff, `vm/run` called `ffi/maybe-run machine register-run-scheduler`, and `register-run-scheduler` used `vm-hot` (which was unguarded then). So no behavior change there — the guard is at the entry points only, \"outside the instruction loop\" as specified.\n\n5. `engine.cljc`: `check-wait-set` gets 2-arity; `scheduler-round-fuel` does check-wait-set then at most one resume. Note the spec says \"After the sweep, resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation.\" `resume-from-run-queue` resumes one entry — and returns the machine with control set but doesn't run it? Let me check `resume-from-run-queue` semantics: in `register-run-scheduler`, `run-loop` calls `resume-from-run-queue` then loops to run the runner. `scheduler-round` (existing) presumably does sweep then resume one. Let me look at the existing `scheduler-round` to compare — it's `(engine/scheduler-round machine register-restore)`. I should check what `scheduler-round` does in the worktree: whether `resume-from-run-queue` executes the resumed activation or just restores it. From the docstring at line 1074-1079, \"One sweep with one budgeted action per child, then at most one resume.\" OK.\n\n6. `frame.cljc`: defect now supports `:run` and contexts `:running`, `:ready`. `window-defect` parameterized by `wanted` set. For run: `(analysis/live (analysis/analyze v) site)` where `site = (:yin.k/pc frame)` — R2 says \"Run window keys equal `L(pc)`\". Note: for run frames, site = pc (not dec pc), and `live` at pc. Looks right per R1/R2.\n\nWait, one thing to check: in C2, for run PC, \"run PC may be zero\". The condition: `(not (and (< n (count v)) (or (= slot :yin.k/site) (and run? (empty? p)) (pos? n))))`. For a run frame at top level (empty? p), zero allowed. For return frames (non-empty p), `(and run? (empty? p))` is false since p non-empty... wait, returns are k frames; they're not run. run? is only true for the top frame. Actually `all` includes the top frame and return frames; return frames are maps with `:yin.k/frame-type :return`, so their `:yin.k/state` is nil, so `run?` is false for them (run? is computed from the top-level frame's state). Hmm wait — run? is bound once from the top frame: `run? (= :run (:yin.k/state frame))`. Inside the `some` over `all`, `f` is each frame but `run?` refers to the top frame's state. For return frames, slot is :yin.k/pc, and `(and run? (empty? p))` — p is non-empty for returns so false; so `(pos? n)` required. Good. But hold on — for the top run frame, if the machine is at pc 0... can pc 0 be a valid run pc? At body start. R2/C2 says \"run PC may be zero\". OK.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: `(and run? (empty? p))` — for a run frame, `(pos? n)` is skipped, so n = 0 allowed, but also need n < count v. And what about n being allowed to equal count v at body end? No, `< n (count v)` still required. Hmm, what about pc at the very end of a body (halting)? A run state at pc = count would be out of bounds. Fine.\n\nAlso C3: `(when-not (or (and run? (empty? p)) (= (code/body-index bs (dec pc)) (code/body-index bs pc)))` — for run, skip body continuity. Note: this `some` is over `envs` which for run? includes all (frame + returns). Wait: `envs (if (or act? run?) all returns)`. So for run, C3's some iterates over all = [frame, returns...]. For the run frame itself, `(and run? (empty? p))` skips. For return frames, normal check. But wait — if run? is true, return frames still get checked with `(dec pc)` — fine.\n\nHmm, but there's an issue: for run, C3's check `(dec pc)` on the *run frame* is skipped, good. But for act frames, the check applies. OK.\n\nNow, the `:site` check for run: In the \"let [v ... site ...]\" block, for run? they do `(window-defect frame v nil (analysis/live ... site) \"\" [])` — no `:site` problem check for run frames. Right, a run frame can be at any pc (any instruction), so no site constraint.\n\nBut wait — there's a potential issue with `window-defect` for run: `(some #(>= % (:registers body)) ks)` — window-scope check: keys must be < (:registers body). For run at pc where body is found via `(code/body-index (code/body-ranges v) pc)`. OK.\n\nOne thing: for run frames, `window-defect` is called with rd=nil, and `(and (some? rd) (contains? w rd))` skips window-self. The doc says \"A run has no window-self analogue.\" Matches.\n\nNow, P1: `(= run? (= :running context))` — run admitted only in :running context. But what enforces \":ready admits only act/tail\"? The condition `(= run? (= :running context))` — if context is :ready and the frame is a run, then run?=true, (= :running :ready)=false → mismatch → problem. Good. And if context is :wait and frame is run → problem. Good.\n\nQ1: for :ready context, reason check uses `(:completion task)` — the completion descriptor passed via task context. Uses `(get-in ... [:yin.k/reason])`. So the completion descriptor is expected to have :yin.k/reason. Need to check rows.cljc for how completion-table maps tags to reasons.\n\nQ2: try? boolean check for :ready context uses completion's `:yin.k/try?`.\n\n7. `layout.cljc`: `stack-layouts` now adds jump-pc layouts: for `:if` nodes, `(assoc (:jump node) (conj pending (result-rd node)))`. And body terminators: `(assoc out (dec (:end body)) [])`. Wait — `(:end body)` — is that the pc after the last instruction? `stack-layout(j) = stack-layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]` per docs. And \"A body's `:return` or `:halt` terminator has layout `[]`.\" Hmm, the terminator pc is `(dec (:end body))`, assoc []. But this is computed BEFORE walking the expr: `(walk (assoc out (dec (:end body)) []) (:expr body) [])`. If the body's last instruction is a :return/:halt, its pc gets []. But what if the terminator pc also gets assoc'd during the walk? The walk assoc's each node's pc. The terminator instruction (:return/:halt) — is it a node in the parse tree expr? Possibly not part of expr tree (it's the body terminator, maybe not in the expression tree). If it were in the tree, the walk would overwrite the `[]`... depends on walk order — walk does `(assoc out (:pc node) pending)` as it goes, so if the terminator node were visited, the pre-seeded `[]` would be overwritten. Presumably terminators aren't in the expr tree. OK.\n\n`result-rd` for :if nodes recurses into `(second (:children node))` — the second child of an if node. Hmm, why? For `stack-layout(j) = stack-layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]`, rd(if) is the rd of the if instruction... Actually `result-rd` gets `code/rd` of the node pc's instruction, but for :if nodes it recurses to the second child. Interesting — maybe because the :if node pc's rd isn't the right one? I can't fully judge without reading layout.cljc in the worktree. The spec says \"For a conditional's jump pc j, stack-layout(j) = stack-layout(b) ++ [rd(if)], where b is its branch pc and both arms define rd(if).\" I'll look at layout_test to see what's pinned.\n\n`lower-defect` in layout.cljc: now inspects slot 17 (run) via state-prim, slot 18 (ready) rows: resolves state cref... wait, look closely:\n\n```clojure\n(some (fn [[i r]]\n        (let [[st c v _ _ refusal] (:slots r)]\n          (or (state-prim code st [:ready i :state])\n              (value-prim code v [:ready i :value])\n              (when refusal (value-prim code refusal [:ready i :refusal]))\n              (some (fn [[j d x]]\n                      (when (= :V d) (value-prim code x [:ready i :completion j])))\n                    (map vector (range) (into [:D] (get rows/completion-table (first (:slots c)))) (:slots c))))))\n      (map-indexed vector (nth slots 18)))\n```\n\nHmm — `(state-prim code st ...)` where st is the first slot of the ready row. The spec says: \"For each slot-18 row it explicitly resolves the first cref to its delivery-state row, then calls `state-prim` on that row.\" Here `st` is the raw slot (a cref, presumably `[:ref ...]` or just the cref id?). `state-prim code st` — does state-prim resolve the cref? The spec said \"explicitly resolves the first cref to its delivery-state row, then calls state-prim on that row.\" If state-prim expects a decoded row (with :kind/:slots), passing a cref would be wrong unless state-prim resolves. Need to check `state-prim` signature in layout.cljc worktree. Also, the destructuring `[st c v _ _ refusal]` assumes the ready row has exactly 6 slots with refusal at index 5. But the grammar says optional-refusal is slot 5 — \"absent unless required\". If the row has fewer slots (e.g., 5 slots without refusal), destructuring gives refusal=nil, fine. But `_ _` skips optional-status (slot 3) and updates-cref (slot 4). What about the updates row body — \"Any value-bearing completion fields that will be restored as backend payload receive the same traversal.\" The update bodies are `:cursor-cell-body` nodes — those contain stream/position... do they need value-prim traversal? The cursor-cell-body for a stream cursor probably contains a position (int) and stream ref, not arbitrary guest values. Hmm, but update bodies are emitted \"directly from its stream and position\". Actually the refusal traversal must cover \"restored value-bearing completion fields\" — for :ffi-request the envelope-V is a value. The `(into [:D] (get rows/completion-table ...))` trick: prepend :D so that the tag itself (slot 0) is treated as :D (data, skipped) and the remaining slots are checked when their descriptor is :V. So envelope-V gets traversed. datom-V in :put gets traversed. Good.\n\nBut wait — the update row (updates-cref, slot 4) is skipped (`_`). Is that OK? The update row is a `:cursor-updates` row (or similar) whose bodies are cursor-cell-body nodes. Those bodies — do they contain values? A cursor cell body is presumably `[:cursor-cell-body stream-ref position]` where stream-ref is a resource ref, not a guest value. So no value-prim needed. But the :ref inside might need... the refusal oracle is about unsupported primitives (register targets refuse certain things). Cursor refs are fine presumably. I'll check rows.cljc encoding of updates.\n\nAlso, does the spec's \"Run windows and lexical bindings\" get covered by `(state-prim code r [:run i])` where r is the slot-17 entry? `(nth slots 17)` — slot 17 is the `:refs :run` row? Wait — \"slots 17 and 18 remain separate ordered references\" — in the task row, slot 17 is `[:refs :run]` and slot 18 is `[:refs :ready]`. So `(nth slots 17)` is a refs row whose... hmm, `(some (fn [[i r]] (state-prim code r [:run i])) (map-indexed vector (nth slots 17)))` — iterating over `(nth slots 17)` directly as a collection of run rows? If slot 17 is a row of kind :refs whose slots are crefs, then `(nth slots 17)` is a node, not a seq. map-indexed over a node would iterate its... hmm. Actually wait — maybe slot 17 of the task's slots is the :refs row's slots vector? Let me check how task rows are decoded. In the existing code `(nth slots 13)` is frames (waits) — `(some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i])) (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))` — so `(nth slots 13)` is directly a sequence of wait rows (each with :slots whose first is the state). So the decoded task structure apparently has slot 13 as a vector of row nodes already (decoded :refs resolve to actual row nodes?). Hmm, but then for slot 17, `state-prim code r` passes r directly — a row node. For ready, `(:slots r)` destructures. OK so for run, r is the run row node and state-prim takes the row node. For ready, st is the first slot of the ready row = a cref to the delivery state... but if :refs decode to row nodes, then st might be the decoded state row node directly. Then `state-prim code st` is consistent. Need to verify in rows.cljc how slot 17/18 decode.\n\nWait, actually there's an inconsistency: for waits `(first (:slots w))` — first slot of the wait row is the state. For ready, `st` = first slot of ready row = delivery state. If wait rows' first slot is a decoded row node, then ready's first slot similarly. Fine.\n\nLet me now continue reading the rows.cljc diff — the biggest chunk (lines 1373-2006).","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Key runtime changes are visible: `run-fuel`, `check-gate!`, frame rules R1/R2/Q1-Q3, layout jump-pc additions, and `lower-defect` ready traversal. Now"}]}}
