uest value. So no value-prim needed. But the :ref inside might need... the refusal oracle is about unsupported primitives (register targets refuse certain things). Cursor refs are fine presumably. I'll check rows.cljc encoding of updates.\n\nAlso, does the spec's \"Run windows and lexical bindings\" get covered by `(state-prim code r [:run i])` where r is the slot-17 entry? `(nth slots 17)` — slot 17 is the `:refs :run` row? Wait — \"slots 17 and 18 remain separate ordered references\" — in the task row, slot 17 is `[:refs :run]` and slot 18 is `[:refs :ready]`. So `(nth slots 17)` is a refs row whose... hmm, `(some (fn [[i r]] (state-prim code r [:run i])) (map-indexed vector (nth slots 17)))` — iterating over `(nth slots 17)` directly as a collection of run rows? If slot 17 is a row of kind :refs whose slots are crefs, then `(nth slots 17)` is a node, not a seq. map-indexed over a node would iterate its... hmm. Actually wait — maybe slot 17 of the task's slots is the :refs row's slots vector? Let me check how task rows are decoded. In the existing code `(nth slots 13)` is frames (waits) — `(some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i])) (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))` — so `(nth slots 13)` is directly a sequence of wait rows (each with :slots whose first is the state). So the decoded task structure apparently has slot 13 as a vector of row nodes already (decoded :refs resolve to actual row nodes?). Hmm, but then for slot 17, `state-prim code r` passes r directly — a row node. For ready, `(:slots r)` destructures. OK so for run, r is the run row node and state-prim takes the row node. For ready, st is the first slot of the ready row = a cref to the delivery state... but if :refs decode to row nodes, then st might be the decoded state row node directly. Then `state-prim code st` is consistent. Need to verify in rows.cljc how slot 17/18 decode.\n\nWait, actually there's an inconsistency: for waits `(first (:slots w))` — first slot of the wait row is the state. For ready, `st` = first slot of ready row = delivery state. If wait rows' first slot is a decoded row node, then ready's first slot similarly. Fine.\n\nLet me now continue reading the rows.cljc diff — the biggest chunk (lines 1373-2006).","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Key runtime changes are visible: `run-fuel`, `check-gate!`, frame rules R1/R2/Q1-Q3, layout jump-pc additions, and `lower-defect` ready traversal. Now the core"}]}}
