harmless).\n\nBut hold on — `:control (select-keys p [:segment :pc])` — the control segment: spec says \"`:control {:segment local :pc pc}`\" — local segment. The restored segment: payload's :segment — is it the local code alias? For waits, entry restore maps segment through aliases presumably. Need to check payload/entry in worktree. The `base` has :code-aliases from attach-module. Presumably payload resolves A → local alias. OK, need verification.\n\n19. **Registry rebinding**:\n```clojure\nmachine (reduce (fn [m [mn entry]]\n                  (if (:address entry)\n                    (engine/receive-module m mn (dissoc entry :bindings)) m))\n                machine (module/module-entries (:modules recv)))]\n```\nThis re-instantiates the receiver's linked modules after carried images/stores. This relates to the doc hunk at line 449-453: \"The receiver's registry pins and slices are kept as given. Its task-local lowered bindings are recreated through engine/receive-module after the carried images and stores, so they name the restored task's code coordinates and carried live stores win.\" Hmm — wait, this doc change was in the section about check-modules!. Is this change part of v2.1 scope? It's not obviously in the v2.1 change list... This might be a fix for \"lowered bindings name stale coordinates\" discovered during implementation. Is it in the base plan (v2) or a mob amendment? Not sure. It's a behavioral change to restore: re-receiving modules the receiver already has. Risk: if receive-module has side effects on stores. The comment says \"receive-module preserves each carried live store\". This is the kind of thing to flag for review — need to check engine/receive-module semantics. Actually wait, this doc text at 449-453 is in the section \"The kinds :role :custody ... are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming\" — that's historical text from module-pins commit (the doc said \"The receiver's registry is kept as given\"). The diff modifies it to describe the