{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the rows.cljc changes. Let me note key observations and things to verify in the worktree:\n\n1. **completion-table**: \n```clojure\n{:next [:C]\n :put [[:ref :stream] :V :optional-D]\n :ffi [:C :D :D]\n :ffi-request [:D :V [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :optional-D]\n :link-request [:D :S :D :optional-D]\n :link-response [:D :S :D]\n :install [:S :D]}\n```\nvs spec §3.1:\n- :next — C ✓\n- :put — stream-cref, datom-V, optional-op-id-D ✓\n- :ffi — C, call-id-D, op-D ✓\n- :ffi-request — call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D ✓\n- :link-request — link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D ✓\n- :link-response — link-id-D, module-S, try?-D ✓\n- :install — module-S, try?-D ✓\nMatches.\n\n2. **row-table**: `:run [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]` ✓; `:ready [[:ref :act :tail] [:ref :completion] :V :optional-D [:ref :cursor-cells] :optional-V]` ✓ matches §3.1 ready grammar. Task adds `[:refs :run] [:refs :ready]` = slots 17, 18 ✓ (task previously had 17 slots 0-16; now 19 slots).\n\n3. **Traversal change**: previously `(= :cursor-cells (:kind n))` skipped ALL cursor-cells rows; now only skips slot 9 of :task (`(not (and (= :task (:kind n)) (= 9 i)))`). So ready update rows (which are :cursor-cells rows referenced from :ready slot 4) get traversed. ✓ matches \"skip the census as a source of ordinary identity occurrences, but traverse ready update keys and bodies as structural references.\"\n\nWait — but in `row` (cell discovery?), the skip is `(when-not (child-task? ctx n) (doseq ... :when (not (and (= :task (:kind n)) (= 9 i)))))`. This means for a :task node, slot 9 (census) is skipped, but other slots traversed. For non-task nodes, all slots traversed — including :cursor-cells rows (ready updates). ✓\n\nBut hold on: what about the *wait* rows' pending? Pending rows reference cursor-cells? Let me think — a pending :next has a C descriptor which is a cell id (data, not ref). The census (:cursor-cells kind) rows map C → body. Ready update rows are also :cursor-cells kind but referenced via `[:ref :cursor-cells]` from :ready rows. When we visit the ready row, we traverse its slots; slot 4 is a ref to a :cursor-cells row; we then traverse that row's slots (pairs of C and cursor-cell-body refs). The C slots have descriptor :C — `(= :C d) (do (when-not (contains? cursor-cells x) (bad! :missing-cursor-cell p)) ...)` — that's in `visit` (canonical numbering check?). OK.\n\n4. **enter-node!** (canonical numbering?): for :task, slot 9 skipped, slots 7/12 (module-store/installs?) sorted; else enter-slot!. For :cursor-cells rows reached via ready, `keyed-structural` — is :cursor-cells in keyed-structural? `:pairs :C [:ref :cursor-cell-body]` — pairs are variadic keyed. `(contains? keyed-structural kind)` — need to check if :cursor-cells is in keyed-structural set. If yes, entries sorted by key and entered as values. OK.\n\n5. **spell-node**: similar: slot 9 → `[:cursor-cells]`. Interesting: `[:cursor-cells]` — presumably the cell id spell marker.\n\n6. **visit** (decoded validation?): `(and (ref? d) (not (and (= :task kind) (= 9 i))))` — hmm, `i` here — is `i` in scope in visit? Look at the code: the cond is inside some fn with `[d x]` and there's `i` from an outer map-indexed? The diff at line 1483-1484: `(and (ref? d) (not= :cursor-cells (:kind x)))` → `(and (ref? d) (not (and (= :task kind) (= 9 i))))`. Need to check `i` binding in the worktree. It's probably `(doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ...)]`. OK.\n\nWait, there's a subtlety: this visit's purpose is checking cursor-cell references: `:C` descriptors must be in the census (`:missing-cursor-cell`). With the change, when visiting a :ready row's update :cursor-cells row, its :C keys are checked against the census ✓ and the :cursor-cell-body refs are visited as refs ✓. And when visiting a :task row, slot 9 is skipped ✓. But when visiting a *non-task* :cursor-cells row... wait, the old code skipped visiting :cursor-cells-kind rows entirely (`(not= :cursor-cells (:kind x))` means don't visit cursor-cells rows). The new code: `(not (and (= :task kind) (= 9 i)))` — visits everything except task slot 9, including :cursor-cells rows reached from ready rows. ✓ consistent.\n\n7. **decode gate**: `(= :task (second row)) (= 19 (count slots))` ✓ 19 slots now.\n\n8. **rows->frame / frame->rows**: handle :run. In `rows->frame` (actually `row->frame`? the fn at 1534), `[seg pc e w k rd]` destructured from slots; for run, slots = [seg pc e w k], so rd = nil. `k (if (or act? run?) k e)` — for tail, slots = [seg site kref], so destructuring [seg pc e w k rd] gives e = kref... and `k (if ... e)` picks e for tail. OK, pre-existing trick.\n\n9. **validate-frames** (judge): \n```clojure\nready (nth slots 18)\ncompleted (map #(second (:slots %)) ready)\nffi-ids (into #{} (keep (fn [w] (let [[_ p] (:slots w)] [reason & xs] (:slots p)] (case reason :ffi (second xs) :ffi-request (first xs) nil)))) (concat waits (map #(node :wait [nil %]) completed)))\n```\nSo FFI call IDs are collected from waits and completed outcomes by wrapping each completion node in a fake :wait node `(node :wait [nil %])` — second slot is the completion. Then `(:slots p)` where p = completion node; `[reason & xs]`. For :ffi `(second xs)` — :ffi slots after tag: [C call-id op] → xs = [C call-id op], second xs = call-id? Wait: xs after tag: `(first xs)` = C, `(second xs)` = call-id. ✓. For :ffi-request: xs = [call-id envelope ...], first = call-id ✓. Matches pending-table convention presumably (:ffi pending has [C ... call-id?]). OK, reuses pending layout since completion-table matches pending-table for :ffi/:ffi-request? Let me check: pending-table isn't shown fully, but :install [:S :D] was shown at top (line 1387) — that's the pending-table's :install? The diff shows completion-table added right after something at line 46-49 that ends with `:install [:S :D]})` — that's pending-table ending. OK.\n\nReady context judging:\n```clojure\n(some (fn [[i r]]\n        (let [[st completion] (:slots r) [tag & xs] (:slots completion)\n              d {:yin.k/reason tag\n                 :yin.k/try? (case tag :install (second xs) (:link-request :link-response) (nth xs 2) nil)}]\n          (judge st :ready (assoc ctx :completion d) [:ready i]))) (map-indexed vector ready))\n```\nBuilds the Q1/Q2 descriptor from the completion row: :yin.k/reason = tag; try? from the right slot. For :link-request xs = [link-id module try? op-id?] → (nth xs 2) = try? ✓. :link-response xs = [link-id module try?] → (nth xs 2) ✓. :install xs = [module try?] → (second xs) ✓.\n\nNote: only state (st) is judged in :ready context; the completion descriptor itself isn't frame-judged (it's data). ✓ \"they do not fabricate a pending field on the delivery frame.\"\n\n10. **check-op-id-shape!** factored ✓; called from check-pendings! slot-18 loop ✓. Q5 diagnostics :malformed-op-id / :inexact via custody-refuse! — need to check custody-refuse! produces :yin.k/undecodable status with those kinds. Spec says diagnostics `:malformed-op-id`, `:inexact` — presumably under :yin.k/undecodable. Fine.\n\nBut wait — check-op-id-shape! checks `(= 2 (count op-id))` map with occurrence + seq. The spec says \"checks the existing two-field operation-ID shape, valid occurrence and exact sequence representation. It does not require root origin, compare the historical occurrence to the current root, or impose the current root's sequence frontier.\" ✓.\n\nBut: \"Every present completed operation ID passes shape-only validation.\" Where are completed op-ids? In :put (optional-op-id-D), :ffi-request (optional-op-id-D), :link-request (optional-op-id-D). The check-pendings! slot-18 loop:\n```clojure\nop-id (case tag :put (nth xs 2) :ffi-request (nth xs 5) :link-request (nth xs 3) nil)\n```\n:put xs = [stream datom op-id?] → nth 2 ✓. :ffi-request xs = [call-id envelope req-stream resp-stream C op-id?] → nth 5 ✓. :link-request xs = [link-id module try? op-id?] → nth 3 ✓. ✓\n\n11. **task-ops / custody-baseline**: `:kind` admits :running ✓. check-task (task-ops?): also checks `(= (if (= :running kind) 1 0) (count (nth (:slots task) 17)))` — running has exactly one run ✓ (wait, this is in custody task-ops? The fn at 1603-1613 with `[ctx task role path]` — that's `check-task` used by task-ops probably).\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety: `(:slots task) 17` — count of runs. For kind :running requires 1 run row; else 0. ✓\n\n12. **check-pendings!** kind rules:\n```clojure\n(when-not (and (= (if (= :running kind) 1 0) (count runs))\n               (= (= :halted kind) (some? result))\n               (or (= :parked kind) (nil? parked-id)))\n  ...)\n(case kind\n  :running nil\n  :halted (when (or (seq waits) (seq ready)) ...)\n  :blocked (when-not (or (seq waits) (seq ready)) ...)\n  :parked (when-not (some #(= parked-id %) ...))\n  ...)\n```\nProduct table: :running — exactly one run, result absent, parked-id absent ✓. But what about :running with... the table says :running can have waits 0..n and ready 0..n — no constraint ✓. :parked — run none? `(= (if (= :running kind) 1 0) (count runs))` — for :parked requires 0 runs ✓; parked-id names table key ✓; result must be absent — `(= (= :halted kind) (some? result))` — for :parked, requires (some? result) = false ✓. :blocked — waits or ready nonempty ✓, runs empty ✓, result absent ✓, parked-id nil ✓. :halted — result present ✓, waits and ready empty ✓, runs empty ✓, parked-id nil ✓.\n\nWait, but what enforces \"waits 0..n\" is fine; what about \":running exactly one run\" combined with... also for :running the row-slot 17 contains exactly one. And what about a :running with parked-id present → `(or (= :parked kind) (nil? parked-id))` fails ✓. \n\nHmm — but one gap: for :blocked, must have empty runs — covered by the count check. OK.\n\nBut wait — does check-pendings! require \"Run is admitted only