comes true. That's at lift; fine.\n\n- completion-node: for :next/:put it delegates to pending-node and reuses its slots! `(node :completion (:slots pending))` — the pending node for :next has slots [:next C ...]? And pending-node may record contexts etc. Interesting reuse. But note the special case: `(if (and (nil? (:reason e)) (:cursor-ref e)) (assoc e :reason :next) e)` — \"apply-observation completes a poll by removing its :observe reason. Its retained cursor identifies the ordinary completed read.\" So a ready entry with nil reason and a cursor-ref becomes :next. Hmm, is this per spec? The spec's completion table has :next for \"Ordinary completed reader\". The engine's apply-observation... this is the poll observation case (acceptance 6 mentions `completed-poll-observation-is-an-ordinary-read-outcome`). OK.\n\n16. **lift-task preflight**: \n```clojure\npreflight (lift-task* m preflight-serve (dissoc opts :serve-keyed) :root)\n_ (doseq [[t _] (task-tree preflight :root)] (check-completions! (nth (:slots t) 18)))\ntask (lift-task* m serve! opts :root)\n```\nPreflight runs lift-task* with temporary identities, no external serve. \"A discharge refusal therefore serves zero handles.\" ✓ matches docs. Note check-completions! runs on preflight (before attachment) ✓ — Q3 status/refusal checks before attachment. Hmm, but wait — check-completions! runs on preflight's ready rows, which are abstract nodes with lifted values. status is `(:status e)` raw value (keyword or nil) ✓; refusal presence via `(when (contains? e :refusal) ...)` — in the row, refusal slot is nil or a lifted value node. `(some? refusal)` in check-completions! checks the slot value non-nil. OK.\n\nBut hmm — check-completions! is called BOTH in lift-task preflight AND in check-pendings! (lower). At lift, refusal before attachment ✓. At lower, check-pendings! runs before attach ✓ (\"These lower refusals occur before attachment\").\n\n17. **Restore (lower-task inner)**: \n```clojure\nready-entries (mapv (fn [r]\n                      (let [[st c v status updates refusal] (:slots r)\n                            [tag & xs] (:slots c)\n                            e (case tag\n                                (:link-request :link-response)\n                                (cond-> (assoc (payload st) :reason tag :link-id (first xs) :name (second xs) :try? (nth xs 2))\n                                  (and (= :link-request tag) (some? (nth xs 3))) (assoc :op-id (nth xs 3)))\n                                :install (assoc (payload st) :reason :install :name (first xs) :try? (second xs))\n                                (entry (node :wait [st c])))]\n                        ...)) ready)\n```\nHmm — for :next/:put/:ffi/:ffi-request, `(entry (node :wait [st c]))` — wraps state+completion as a fake :wait row and runs the existing wait-entry restore! Since completion-table mirrors pending-table for those tags (spec: \":ffi and :ffi-request wire tags preserve the existing distinction\"), the pending restore path reconstructs cursor-ref/stream-id etc. Clever. Need to verify `entry` (the wait-entry restore fn) handles this: it takes a :wait row [state pending]. The completion row's slots match pending row's slots for :next/:put/:ffi/:ffi-request? \n\npending-table :next vs completion-table :next [:C] ✓ presumably same.\n:put pending: probably [[:ref :stream] :V :optional-D] ✓ same as completion.\n:ffi pending: [:C :D :D] ✓.\n:ffi-request pending: [:D :V [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :optional-D] ✓ presumably.\n\nAnd \"A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition\" — the pending :ffi-request restore creates an entry with :request-sent true. ✓ via entry.\n\nThen:\n```clojure\n(cond-> (assoc e :value (value v) :status status\n               :resource-updates (into {} (map (fn [[c b]]\n                                                 [(get cell-keys c)\n                                                  {:stream-id (stream-key (first (:slots b))) :cursor (second (:slots b))}]))\n                                           (partition 2 (:slots updates))))\n  (some? refusal) (assoc :refusal (value refusal)))\n```\nResource updates restored with keys mapped through cell-keys (C → fresh resource key) ✓ and bodies from the ready row ✓ (\"update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9\").\n\nHmm wait — what about :refusal restore for link-refused: \"restore reinstates :refusal so the existing terminal-resume path raises the same error\". `(some? refusal) (assoc :refusal (value refusal))` ✓. And :status :link-refused restored ✓.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: `(:status e)` at lift — when status is absent it's nil; at restore `:status status` sets nil explicitly. Engine entries with nil status — fine.\n\n18. **:running restore**:\n```clojure\n:running (let [p (dissoc (payload (first runs)) :deliver)]\n           (assoc machine :control (select-keys p [:segment :pc]) :env (:env p)\n                  :window (:window p) :k (when (seq (:k p)) (:k p))\n                  :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries))\n```\nSpec: `{:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries}` ✓.\n\nBut `(payload (first runs))` — payload decodes a state row to engine state fields? `payload` presumably converts a decoded act/tail/run row into the engine payload map {:segment :pc :env :window :k ...}. The run row decodes via rows->frame into {:yin.k/state :run ...}. Hmm, wait — does payload take a decoded frame or a row node? `(payload st)` is used for link/install too where st is the state row node of the ready row. So payload takes a row node and returns engine-shaped map. And dissoc :deliver since run has none anyway (rows->frame for run doesn't assoc :yin.k/deliver... wait it does NOT for run: `(not run?) (assoc :yin.k/deliver ...)` — so run frames have no :yin.k/deliver key. But payload might produce :deliver for act/tail;