{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the rows.cljc changes. Let me note key observations and things to verify in the worktree:\n\n1. **completion-table**: \n```clojure\n{:next [:C]\n :put [[:ref :stream] :V :optional-D]\n :ffi [:C :D :D]\n :ffi-request [:D :V [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :optional-D]\n :link-request [:D :S :D :optional-D]\n :link-response [:D :S :D]\n :install [:S :D]}\n```\nvs spec §3.1:\n- :next — C ✓\n- :put — stream-cref, datom-V, optional-op-id-D ✓\n- :ffi — C, call-id-D, op-D ✓\n- :ffi-request — call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D ✓\n- :link-request — link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D ✓\n- :link-response — link-id-D, module-S, try?-D ✓\n- :install — module-S, try?-D ✓\nMatches.\n\n2. **row-table**: `:run [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]` ✓; `:ready [[:ref :act :tail] [:ref :completion] :V :optional-D [:ref :cursor-cells] :optional-V]` ✓ matches §3.1 ready grammar. Task adds `[:refs :run] [:refs :ready]` = slots 17, 18 ✓ (task previously had 17 slots 0-16; now 19 slots).\n\n3. **Traversal change**: previously `(= :cursor-cells (:kind n))` skipped ALL cursor-cells rows; now only skips slot 9 of :task (`(not (and (= :task (:kind n)) (= 9 i)))`). So ready update rows (which are :cursor-cells rows referenced from :ready slot 4) get traversed. ✓ matches \"skip the census as a source of ordinary identity occurrences, but traverse ready update keys and bodies as structural references.\"\n\nWait — but in `row` (cell discovery?), the skip is `(when-not (child-task? ctx n) (doseq ... :when (not (and (= :task (:kind n)) (= 9 i)))))`. This means for a :task node, slot 9 (census) is skipped, but other slots traversed. For non-task nodes, all slots traversed — including :cursor-cells rows (ready updates). ✓\n\nBut hold on: what about the *wait* rows' pending? Pending rows reference cursor-cells? Let me think — a pending :next has a C descriptor which is a cell id (data, not ref). The census (:cursor-cells kind) rows map C → body. Ready update rows are also :cursor-cells kind but referenced via `[:ref :cursor-cells]` from :ready rows. When we visit the ready row, we traverse its slots; slot 4 is a ref to a :cursor-cells row; we then traverse that row's slots (pairs of C and cursor-cell-body refs). The C slots have descriptor :C — `(= :C d) (do (when-not (contains? cursor-cells x) (bad! :missing-cursor-cell p)) ...)` — that's in `visit` (canonical numbering check?). OK.\n\n4. **enter-node!** (canonical numbering?): for :task, slot 9 skipped, slots 7/12 (module-store/installs?) sorted; else enter-slot!. For :cursor-cells rows reached via ready, `keyed-structural` — is :cursor-cells in keyed-structural? `:pairs :C [:ref :cursor-cell-body]` — pairs are variadic keyed. `(contains? keyed-structural kind)` — need to check if :cursor-cells is in keyed-structural set. If yes, entries sorted by key and entered as values. OK.\n\n5. **spell-node**: similar: slot 9 → `[:cursor-cells]`. Interesting: `[:cursor-cells]` — presumably the cell id spell marker.\n\n6. **visit** (decoded validation?): `(and (ref? d) (not (and (= :task kind) (= 9 i))))` — hmm, `i` here — is `i` in scope in visit? Look at the code: the cond is inside some fn with `[d x]` and there's `i` from an outer map-indexed? The diff at line 1483-1484: `(and (ref? d) (not= :cursor-cells (:kind x)))` → `(and (ref? d) (not (and (= :task kind) (= 9 i))))`. Need to check `i` binding in the worktree. It's probably `(doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ...)]`. OK.\n\nWait, there's a subtlety: this visit's purpose is checking cursor-cell references: `:C` descriptors must be in the census (`:missing-cursor-cell`). With the change, when visiting a :ready row's update :cursor-cells row, its :C keys are checked against the census ✓ and the :cursor-cell-body refs are visited as refs ✓. And when visiting a :task row, slot 9 is skipped ✓. But when visiting a *non-task* :cursor-cells row... wait, the old code skipped visiting :cursor-cells-kind rows entirely (`(not= :cursor-cells (:kind x))` means don't visit cursor-cells rows). The new code: `(not (and (= :task kind) (= 9 i)))` — visits everything except task slot 9, including :cursor-cells rows reached from ready rows. ✓ consistent.\n\n7. **decode gate**: `(= :task (second row)) (= 19 (count slots))` ✓ 19 slots now.\n\n8. **rows->frame / frame->rows**: handle :run. In `rows->frame` (actually `row->frame`? the fn at 1534), `[seg pc e w k rd]` destructured from slots; for run, slots = [seg pc e w k], so rd = nil. `k (if (or act? run?) k e)` — for tail, slots = [seg site kref], so destructuring [seg pc e w k rd] gives e = kref... and `k (if ... e)` picks e for tail. OK, pre-existing trick.\n\n9. **validate-frames** (judge): \n```clojure\nready (nth slots 18)\ncompleted (map #(second (:slots %)) ready)\nffi-ids (into #{} (keep (fn [w] (let [[_ p] (:slots w)] [reason & xs] (:slots p)] (case reason :ffi (second xs) :ffi-request (first xs) nil)))) (concat waits (map #(node :wait [nil %]) completed)))\n```\nSo FFI call IDs are collected from waits and completed outcomes by wrapping each completion node in a fake :wait node `(node :wait [nil %])` — second slot is the completion. Then `(:slots p)` where p = completion node; `[reason & xs]`. For :ffi `(second xs)` — :ffi slots after tag: [C call-id op] → xs = [C call-id op], second xs = call-id? Wait: xs after tag: `(first xs)` = C, `(second xs)` = call-id. ✓. For :ffi-request: xs = [call-id envelope ...], first = call-id ✓. Matches pending-table convention presumably (:ffi pending has [C ... call-id?]). OK, reuses pending layout since completion-table matches pending-table for :ffi/:ffi-request? Let me check: pending-table isn't shown fully, but :install [:S :D] was shown at top (line 1387) — that's the pending-table's :install? The diff shows completion-table added right after something at line 46-49 that ends with `:install [:S :D]})` — that's pending-table ending. OK.\n\nReady context judging:\n```clojure\n(some (fn [[i r]]\n        (let [[st completion] (:slots r) [tag & xs] (:slots completion)\n              d {:yin.k/reason tag\n                 :yin.k/try? (case tag :install (second xs) (:link-request :link-response) (nth xs 2) nil)}]\n          (judge st :ready (assoc ctx :completion d) [:ready i]))) (map-indexed vector ready))\n```\nBuilds the Q1/Q2 descriptor from the completion row: :yin.k/reason = tag; try? from the right slot. For :link-request xs = [link-id module try? op-id?] → (nth xs 2) = try? ✓. :link-response xs = [link-id module try?] → (nth xs 2) ✓. :install xs = [module try?] → (second xs) ✓.\n\nNote: only state (st) is judged in :ready context; the completion descriptor itself isn't frame-judged (it's data). ✓ \"they do not fabricate a pending field on the delivery frame.\"\n\n10. **check-op-id-shape!** factored ✓; called from check-pendings! slot-18 loop ✓. Q5 diagnostics :malformed-op-id / :inexact via custody-refuse! — need to check custody-refuse! produces :yin.k/undecodable status with those kinds. Spec says diagnostics `:malformed-op-id`, `:inexact` — presumably under :yin.k/undecodable. Fine.\n\nBut wait — check-op-id-shape! checks `(= 2 (count op-id))` map with occurrence + seq. The spec says \"checks the existing two-field operation-ID shape, valid occurrence and exact sequence representation. It does not require root origin, compare the historical occurrence to the current root, or impose the current root's sequence frontier.\" ✓.\n\nBut: \"Every present completed operation ID passes shape-only validation.\" Where are completed op-ids? In :put (optional-op-id-D), :ffi-request (optional-op-id-D), :link-request (optional-op-id-D). The check-pendings! slot-18 loop:\n```clojure\nop-id (case tag :put (nth xs 2) :ffi-request (nth xs 5) :link-request (nth xs 3) nil)\n```\n:put xs = [stream datom op-id?] → nth 2 ✓. :ffi-request xs = [call-id envelope req-stream resp-stream C op-id?] → nth 5 ✓. :link-request xs = [link-id module try? op-id?] → nth 3 ✓. ✓\n\n11. **task-ops / custody-baseline**: `:kind` admits :running ✓. check-task (task-ops?): also checks `(= (if (= :running kind) 1 0) (count (nth (:slots task) 17)))` — running has exactly one run ✓ (wait, this is in custody task-ops? The fn at 1603-1613 with `[ctx task role path]` — that's `check-task` used by task-ops probably).\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety: `(:slots task) 17` — count of runs. For kind :running requires 1 run row; else 0. ✓\n\n12. **check-pendings!** kind rules:\n```clojure\n(when-not (and (= (if (= :running kind) 1 0) (count runs))\n               (= (= :halted kind) (some? result))\n               (or (= :parked kind) (nil? parked-id)))\n  ...)\n(case kind\n  :running nil\n  :halted (when (or (seq waits) (seq ready)) ...)\n  :blocked (when-not (or (seq waits) (seq ready)) ...)\n  :parked (when-not (some #(= parked-id %) ...))\n  ...)\n```\nProduct table: :running — exactly one run, result absent, parked-id absent ✓. But what about :running with... the table says :running can have waits 0..n and ready 0..n — no constraint ✓. :parked — run none? `(= (if (= :running kind) 1 0) (count runs))` — for :parked requires 0 runs ✓; parked-id names table key ✓; result must be absent — `(= (= :halted kind) (some? result))` — for :parked, requires (some? result) = false ✓. :blocked — waits or ready nonempty ✓, runs empty ✓, result absent ✓, parked-id nil ✓. :halted — result present ✓, waits and ready empty ✓, runs empty ✓, parked-id nil ✓.\n\nWait, but what enforces \"waits 0..n\" is fine; what about \":running exactly one run\" combined with... also for :running the row-slot 17 contains exactly one. And what about a :running with parked-id present → `(or (= :parked kind) (nil? parked-id))` fails ✓. \n\nHmm — but one gap: for :blocked, must have empty runs — covered by the count check. OK.\n\nBut wait — does check-pendings! require \"Run is admitted only in :running\" — that's P1 in frame validation; the kind check here covers cardinality. ✓\n\n13. **Fenced restore**: `apply-gate` (the fn at 2211-2218) now also retains `:yin.k/recovery-ready (:ready-queue m) :ready-queue []` ✓ matches \"A fenced restore retains ready entries in :yin.k/recovery-ready\".\n\n14. **lift-task\\*** recovery handling:\n```clojure\n(let [m (cond-> m\n          (contains? m :yin.k/recovery-waits) (assoc :wait-set (:yin.k/recovery-waits m))\n          (contains? m :yin.k/recovery-ready) (assoc :ready-queue (:yin.k/recovery-ready m))\n          (contains? m :yin.k/recovery-parked) (assoc :parked (:yin.k/recovery-parked m)))]\n```\nSo relift of a fenced machine swaps recovery data back in for lifting. This gives byte-identical relift ✓.\n\nKind classification: `(cond (some? (:control m)) :running active? :parked (or (seq (:wait-set m)) (seq (:ready-queue m))) :blocked (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? m) :halted :else (outcome! ... :inconsistent-state))`. Precedence running → parked → blocked → halted ✓ matches §3.1. The else used to be not-at-safepoint; now :inconsistent-state. Docs say \"anything else is :yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state (unreachable by construction)\" ✓.\n\nWait — but what about halted with waits/ready? Per table, halted activation beside queued work classifies :blocked. In this classification, if control nil and (seq wait-set) → :blocked even if halted? is true ✓ (\"A halted activation beside queued work classifies :blocked\"). And halted with outstanding waits and empty queue... \"A halted activation with outstanding waits and an empty queue also classifies :blocked\" — covered by (seq wait-set) → :blocked ✓, dropping the halt value (result is only lifted when kind = :halted) ✓.\n\nAnd \"halted\" requires halted-with-empty-queue? — but now must also have empty ready-queue? The cond reaches the halted branch only when wait-set AND ready-queue are both empty. `engine/halted-with-empty-queue?` presumably checks :halted? and empty ready-queue (pre-existing). ✓\n\nRun construction:\n```clojure\nruns (if (= :running kind)\n       (let [{:keys [segment pc]} (:control m)\n             a (get-in m [:code segment :analysis])]\n         [(state-node ctx {:yin.k/state :run :segment segment :pc pc :env (:env m)\n                           :window (select-keys (:window m) (analysis/live a pc)) :k (:k m)})]) [])\n```\nWindow trimmed to (analysis/live a pc) = L(pc) ✓ per §3.2 step 3. Hmm — is `analysis` aliased in rows.cljc? Need to verify the ns. Also `(get-in m [:code segment :analysis])` — the analysis is per segment from the machine's code. Need to verify :analysis key exists in machine code entries. Plausible.\n\n15. **ready-node**:\n```clojure\n(node :ready [(state-node ctx e) (completion-node ctx e) (lift-value ctx (:value e)) (:status e)\n              (node :cursor-cells\n                    (mapcat (fn [[c body]]\n                              [(cell-for! ctx c)\n                               (node :cursor-cell-body [(stream-row! ctx (:stream-id body)) (:cursor body)])])\n                            (:resource-updates e)))\n              (when (contains? e :refusal) (lift-value ctx (:refusal e)))])\n```\n- slot 0: delivery state via state-node ✓ (the ready entry e has :segment/:pc etc? Actually wait entries have state fields at top level — state-node handles :yin.k/state :run case and :site (tail) / else act. A ready entry from engine has what shape? Engine ready entries are maps like {:reason ... :segment ... :pc ...}? Need to check engine's make-woken-run-queue-entries. The ready entry must carry the delivery state fields. state-node checks `(:yin.k/state p)` = :run first, then `(contains? p :site)` for tail, else act with `:deliver :rd`. So engine ready entries have :segment/:pc/:env/:window/:k/:deliver. OK.)\n\n- slot 3: `(:status e)` — if absent, nil. Row slot is :optional-D, so nil encodes as absent? Need to check how :optional-D encodes nil — presumably nil slot is dropped or encoded as nil marker. Hmm — \"Empty or nil update maps have one spelling: an empty update row\" and \"status may be absent: it is encoded canonically as nil\". Let me check how node emission handles nil for :optional-D. There might be an issue if (:status e) is nil but the slot must still be present as a placeholder since slot 4 (updates) and slot 5 (refusal) follow. For :optional-D, nil likely encodes as some nil literal. And :optional-V with nil (when (contains? e :refusal) ...) → nil when no refusal. Need to check emit handles nil slots for :optional-*. The row grammar has fixed arity 6 for :ready, so slots 3,4,5 always present in the abstract row; encoding of nil optional-D is presumably `[:lit nil]`? or a special nil? I'll check `enter-slot!`/emit handling of nil for :optional-D in the worktree.\n\n- The update row: `(node :cursor-cells (mapcat (fn [[c body]] [(cell-for! ctx c) (node :cursor-cell-body ...)])) ...)` — registers/resolves the resource identity through the task census via `cell-for!`, then emits the update's own `:cursor-cell-body` node directly from stream+position, NOT via a second `cell!` call. ✓ matches spec: \"Register or resolve the same resource identity through the task census. Emit the update's own :cursor-cell-body node directly from its stream and position. Pair that node with the same canonical C in the ready update row. Do not register the update body through a second cell! call.\"\n\nNeed to check `cell-for!` — spec says \"cell-for! registers each referenced resource identity with its current machine resource body.\" Is that the existing `cell!` or a new function? In the old code there was `cell!`. The diff shows `cell-for!` — is it defined in the diff? I haven't seen its definition. Let me grep. Also `stream-row!`.\n\n- `(boolean (:try? e))` in completion-node for link/install — coerces try? to boolean. Hmm — spec Q2 says \"Link/install completion try? is boolean\". Coercing with `boolean` at lift means a truthy non-boolean try? becomes true. That's at lift; fine.\n\n- completion-node: for :next/:put it delegates to pending-node and reuses its slots! `(node :completion (:slots pending))` — the pending node for :next has slots [:next C ...]? And pending-node may record contexts etc. Interesting reuse. But note the special case: `(if (and (nil? (:reason e)) (:cursor-ref e)) (assoc e :reason :next) e)` — \"apply-observation completes a poll by removing its :observe reason. Its retained cursor identifies the ordinary completed read.\" So a ready entry with nil reason and a cursor-ref becomes :next. Hmm, is this per spec? The spec's completion table has :next for \"Ordinary completed reader\". The engine's apply-observation... this is the poll observation case (acceptance 6 mentions `completed-poll-observation-is-an-ordinary-read-outcome`). OK.\n\n16. **lift-task preflight**: \n```clojure\npreflight (lift-task* m preflight-serve (dissoc opts :serve-keyed) :root)\n_ (doseq [[t _] (task-tree preflight :root)] (check-completions! (nth (:slots t) 18)))\ntask (lift-task* m serve! opts :root)\n```\nPreflight runs lift-task* with temporary identities, no external serve. \"A discharge refusal therefore serves zero handles.\" ✓ matches docs. Note check-completions! runs on preflight (before attachment) ✓ — Q3 status/refusal checks before attachment. Hmm, but wait — check-completions! runs on preflight's ready rows, which are abstract nodes with lifted values. status is `(:status e)` raw value (keyword or nil) ✓; refusal presence via `(when (contains? e :refusal) ...)` — in the row, refusal slot is nil or a lifted value node. `(some? refusal)` in check-completions! checks the slot value non-nil. OK.\n\nBut hmm — check-completions! is called BOTH in lift-task preflight AND in check-pendings! (lower). At lift, refusal before attachment ✓. At lower, check-pendings! runs before attach ✓ (\"These lower refusals occur before attachment\").\n\n17. **Restore (lower-task inner)**: \n```clojure\nready-entries (mapv (fn [r]\n                      (let [[st c v status updates refusal] (:slots r)\n                            [tag & xs] (:slots c)\n                            e (case tag\n                                (:link-request :link-response)\n                                (cond-> (assoc (payload st) :reason tag :link-id (first xs) :name (second xs) :try? (nth xs 2))\n                                  (and (= :link-request tag) (some? (nth xs 3))) (assoc :op-id (nth xs 3)))\n                                :install (assoc (payload st) :reason :install :name (first xs) :try? (second xs))\n                                (entry (node :wait [st c])))]\n                        ...)) ready)\n```\nHmm — for :next/:put/:ffi/:ffi-request, `(entry (node :wait [st c]))` — wraps state+completion as a fake :wait row and runs the existing wait-entry restore! Since completion-table mirrors pending-table for those tags (spec: \":ffi and :ffi-request wire tags preserve the existing distinction\"), the pending restore path reconstructs cursor-ref/stream-id etc. Clever. Need to verify `entry` (the wait-entry restore fn) handles this: it takes a :wait row [state pending]. The completion row's slots match pending row's slots for :next/:put/:ffi/:ffi-request? \n\npending-table :next vs completion-table :next [:C] ✓ presumably same.\n:put pending: probably [[:ref :stream] :V :optional-D] ✓ same as completion.\n:ffi pending: [:C :D :D] ✓.\n:ffi-request pending: [:D :V [:ref :stream] [:ref :stream] :C :optional-D] ✓ presumably.\n\nAnd \"A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition\" — the pending :ffi-request restore creates an entry with :request-sent true. ✓ via entry.\n\nThen:\n```clojure\n(cond-> (assoc e :value (value v) :status status\n               :resource-updates (into {} (map (fn [[c b]]\n                                                 [(get cell-keys c)\n                                                  {:stream-id (stream-key (first (:slots b))) :cursor (second (:slots b))}]))\n                                           (partition 2 (:slots updates))))\n  (some? refusal) (assoc :refusal (value refusal)))\n```\nResource updates restored with keys mapped through cell-keys (C → fresh resource key) ✓ and bodies from the ready row ✓ (\"update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9\").\n\nHmm wait — what about :refusal restore for link-refused: \"restore reinstates :refusal so the existing terminal-resume path raises the same error\". `(some? refusal) (assoc :refusal (value refusal))` ✓. And :status :link-refused restored ✓.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: `(:status e)` at lift — when status is absent it's nil; at restore `:status status` sets nil explicitly. Engine entries with nil status — fine.\n\n18. **:running restore**:\n```clojure\n:running (let [p (dissoc (payload (first runs)) :deliver)]\n           (assoc machine :control (select-keys p [:segment :pc]) :env (:env p)\n                  :window (:window p) :k (when (seq (:k p)) (:k p))\n                  :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries))\n```\nSpec: `{:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries}` ✓.\n\nBut `(payload (first runs))` — payload decodes a state row to engine state fields? `payload` presumably converts a decoded act/tail/run row into the engine payload map {:segment :pc :env :window :k ...}. The run row decodes via rows->frame into {:yin.k/state :run ...}. Hmm, wait — does payload take a decoded frame or a row node? `(payload st)` is used for link/install too where st is the state row node of the ready row. So payload takes a row node and returns engine-shaped map. And dissoc :deliver since run has none anyway (rows->frame for run doesn't assoc :yin.k/deliver... wait it does NOT for run: `(not run?) (assoc :yin.k/deliver ...)` — so run frames have no :yin.k/deliver key. But payload might produce :deliver for act/tail; for run it wouldn't. The dissoc is harmless).\n\nBut hold on — `:control (select-keys p [:segment :pc])` — the control segment: spec says \"`:control {:segment local :pc pc}`\" — local segment. The restored segment: payload's :segment — is it the local code alias? For waits, entry restore maps segment through aliases presumably. Need to check payload/entry in worktree. The `base` has :code-aliases from attach-module. Presumably payload resolves A → local alias. OK, need verification.\n\n19. **Registry rebinding**:\n```clojure\nmachine (reduce (fn [m [mn entry]]\n                  (if (:address entry)\n                    (engine/receive-module m mn (dissoc entry :bindings)) m))\n                machine (module/module-entries (:modules recv)))]\n```\nThis re-instantiates the receiver's linked modules after carried images/stores. This relates to the doc hunk at line 449-453: \"The receiver's registry pins and slices are kept as given. Its task-local lowered bindings are recreated through engine/receive-module after the carried images and stores, so they name the restored task's code coordinates and carried live stores win.\" Hmm — wait, this doc change was in the section about check-modules!. Is this change part of v2.1 scope? It's not obviously in the v2.1 change list... This might be a fix for \"lowered bindings name stale coordinates\" discovered during implementation. Is it in the base plan (v2) or a mob amendment? Not sure. It's a behavioral change to restore: re-receiving modules the receiver already has. Risk: if receive-module has side effects on stores. The comment says \"receive-module preserves each carried live store\". This is the kind of thing to flag for review — need to check engine/receive-module semantics. Actually wait, this doc text at 449-453 is in the section \"The kinds :role :custody ... are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming\" — that's historical text from module-pins commit (the doc said \"The receiver's registry is kept as given\"). The diff modifies it to describe the new rebinding behavior. This could be one of the \"two items flagged for architect confirmation\"? No — the two flagged items are row 20 exclusions and row 17 like-cases. Hmm, so this registry rebinding is an unflagged behavior change. Let me check the engineer report again... The report's attribution table doesn't mention it explicitly. Hmm, wait — actually let me re-read: \"the codex part of the docs listed below\". The doc hunk at line 449-453 of the diff (\"The receiver's registry pins and slices are kept as given. Its task-local lowered bindings are recreated through engine/receive-module...\") — this is in yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md. So it's a deliberate change with doc sync. It may be from module-pins-related fix or a restore-order bug found when running children lift/lower (item 10: \"lift/lower running children under receiver admission; continue to linked\"). The receive-module rebinding likely ensures the restored task can `require` modules the receiver already had linked, with carried live stores winning. I should look at engine/receive-module to assess risk.\n\n20. **Malformed-rows test fixtures** and other tests — will read next.\n\nAlso need to check: `unliftable-hold` still refuses holds ✓ (kept). The ready-queue refusal removed ✓.\n\nOne thing to verify: **`(boolean (:try? e))`** — for :link-request, spec says `try?-D`. If engine entry has try? true/false ✓. For a *successful* link completion, what is try?? Engine link entries have :try? field. OK.\n\n**Q3 check**: status nil or keyword; :link-refused requires link/install tag + try? false + refusal present; other statuses → refusal must be nil. Hmm wait — \"other outcomes have no refusal slot\" — the check `(nil? refusal)` for non-:link-refused statuses. But what about a :next/:put/:ffi outcome with status non-nil non-:link-refused and refusal present → refused ✓. But what about try failure outcomes: \"A try failure travels in :V as ordinary returned outcome data\" — status absent (nil) presumably, refusal nil ✓.\n\nBut subtle: what about `:link-refused` status on a :next/:put completion → refused ✓ (tag not in link set).\n\n21. Now, **concern: `run-fuel` bridge dispatch**. `(if (and (:blocked? v) (ffi/installed? v)) (:vm (ffi/bridge-step v)) v)`. Spec: \"If the result is blocked and an explicit bridge is installed, at most one ffi/bridge-step.\" ✓. But note: `ffi/bridge-step` returns {:vm ...}? It destructures :vm. OK.\n\nAlso `run-fuel` child invocation: `(engine/scheduler-round-fuel machine register-restore #(run-fuel % n))` — children get `run-fuel child n` ✓ \"Each visited child is invoked once through run-fuel child n\".\n\nBut wait — in scheduler-round-fuel → check-wait-set → poll-links → advance-installs run-child → advance-install applies `run-child` to the install child = `#(run-fuel % n)` ✓. But `advance-install` wraps in try/catch and `stamp-gate`. ✓ \"Preserve existing gate stamping and the prohibition on running children under closed gates\" — `(when-not (contains? #{:exporting :ended} mode) ...)` in advance-install ✓.\n\nHmm, but one question: in the budgeted scheduler round, after sweep \"resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation.\" `resume-from-run-queue polled restore-fn` — does resume-from-run-queue execute? Looking at existing `scheduler-round` at line 1097-1098: `(engine/scheduler-round machine register-restore)`. Let me check engine/scheduler-round in the worktree — it probably does `(or (resume-from-run-queue (check-wait-set state) restore-fn) ...)`. And resume-from-run-queue restores the activation into control but doesn't execute (execution happens in run-loop). In scheduler-round-fuel, after resume the machine is returned with control set — not executed ✓.\n\n22. **vm/step gate check**: `(step [machine] (check-gate! machine) (telemetry/emit-snapshot ...))`. Wait — check-gate! is called, THEN emit-snapshot evaluates `(if (engine/ready-for-ingress? machine) machine (vm-hot* machine 1))`. Order fine.\n\nBut hmm — vm/step's telemetry: \"vm/step keeps its telemetry behavior\" ✓.\n\n23. **run-fuel arg validation**: `(code/reg? n)` — need to check code/reg? exists and means exact non-negative integer... reg? probably checks register indices (exact non-negative integer). With pos? → positive exact integer ✓. Is `code` aliased in semantic.cljc? The ns shows requires: dao.jing, dao.stream.apply, yin.vm... the diff doesn't show the full ns. Need to check the worktree for `[yin.vm.semantic.code :as code]` and `[yin.vm.semantic.analysis :as analysis]`... semantic.cljc line 1097 shows `(engine/scheduler-round machine register-restore)`. I'll verify requires.\n\nAlso **does `vm-hot*`'s old docstring/behavior stay** — nil fuel runs to exit ✓ unchanged.\n\n24. **`check-gate!` covers `run-fuel`** ✓, `vm-hot` ✓, `step` ✓, `run` ✓. What about the protocol's other entries? `eval` calls `vm/run` → guarded ✓.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue: `vm/run` on a fenced machine: check-gate! throws. What did the old behavior do on gated machines? `ffi/maybe-run` probably checked gates? The spec says the guard is new in 6d-a. Previously running a gated machine may have been possible? Not relevant — spec wants refusal.\n\nWhat refusal shape do the tests assert? Need to check vm_test or safepoint_harness fuel-and-closed-gates test. The ex-info is `{:rule :gate-closed :yin.k/gate mode}` — no :yin.k/status. Is that per spec? Spec doesn't name a diagnostic for the guard, just \"refuses :exporting and :ended\". Hmm — \"refuses the closed gate before guest, scheduler or bridge work\". The tests presumably assert thrown ex-info with :rule :gate-closed. I'll check the test.\n\n25. **Ledger golden & rows-checkpoint fixture diffs** — need to verify the ledger-v2.txt diff is address-only per ruling 3. Lines 2006-2039 (33 lines) and rows-checkpoint lines 2039-3497 (1458 lines!). The rows-checkpoint fixture has huge lines. Let me read those