The diff modifies it to describe the new rebinding behavior. This could be one of the \"two items flagged for architect confirmation\"? No — the two flagged items are row 20 exclusions and row 17 like-cases. Hmm, so this registry rebinding is an unflagged behavior change. Let me check the engineer report again... The report's attribution table doesn't mention it explicitly. Hmm, wait — actually let me re-read: \"the codex part of the docs listed below\". The doc hunk at line 449-453 of the diff (\"The receiver's registry pins and slices are kept as given. Its task-local lowered bindings are recreated through engine/receive-module...\") — this is in yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md. So it's a deliberate change with doc sync. It may be from module-pins-related fix or a restore-order bug found when running children lift/lower (item 10: \"lift/lower running children under receiver admission; continue to linked\"). The receive-module rebinding likely ensures the restored task can `require` modules the receiver already had linked, with carried live stores winning. I should look at engine/receive-module to assess risk.\n\n20. **Malformed-rows test fixtures** and other tests — will read next.\n\nAlso need to check: `unliftable-hold` still refuses holds ✓ (kept). The ready-queue refusal removed ✓.\n\nOne thing to verify: **`(boolean (:try? e))`** — for :link-request, spec says `try?-D`. If engine entry has try? true/false ✓. For a *successful* link completion, what is try?? Engine link entries have :try? field. OK.\n\n**Q3 check**: status nil or keyword; :link-refused requires link/install tag + try? false + refusal present; other statuses → refusal must be nil. Hmm wait — \"other outcomes have no refusal slot\" — the check `(nil? refusal)` for non-:link-refused statuses. But what about a :next/:put/:ffi outcome with status non-nil non-:link-refused and refusal present → refused ✓. But what about try failure outcomes: \"A try failure travels in :V as ordinary returned outcome