lots 17] into (slot t 17))` — appends the run again → count 2 → :kind ✓.\n\n19. **rows_test `running-task-round-trip-and-fencing`** — byte-identical relift via root equality `(is (= (:yin.k/root wire) (:yin.k/root again)))` ✓ and fenced `= wire (support/lift fenced nil)` ✓ and gate refusals ✓. Mutation matrix refuses :kind ✓. `(is (zero? @n))` — hmm, for the FIRST lower (recv), `(is (zero? @n))`?? Wait — `(is (zero? @n))` after `recv (rows/lower-task ...)` — lowering a running task attaches nothing? An app of lam to nil — pure, no streams in the task → zero attaches ✓ makes sense.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's an issue: `(= wire (support/lift fenced nil))` — full wire equality including bytes ✓ good.\n\n20. **rows-custody exclusive-completed-write-is-history** ✓ — completed :put excluded from custody, not re-appended, value delivered.\n\nHmm — wait, one thing to check: the completed write op-id restore `(is (= (s/op-id 0) (:op-id (first (:ready-queue recv)))))` ✓ carried as history.\n\n21. **registry rebind**: in lower, after machine built, `(reduce (fn [m [mn entry]] (if (:address entry) (engine/receive-module m mn (dissoc entry :bindings)) m)) machine (module/module-entries (:modules recv)))`. Need to check `engine/receive-module` and `module/module-entries` in worktree to assess. The concern: re-receiving every linked module on every lower — could this duplicate appends or reset stores? Comment says \"receive-module preserves each carried live store\". Also it could affect :id-counter or ready-queue? Need to read engine/receive-module in worktree.\n\nAlso this could break law 2 (immediate relift byte equality) if receive-module changes module stores non-deterministically — but tests assert relift equality on link/install cases (completed-link-and-install-outcomes asserts... hmm, it asserts source-outcome = received-outcome and slot 18 count, not relift equality. But boundary-round-trip asserts relift canonical-under equality for every case — canonical-under normalizes fresh identities). And direct-transfer asserts `(= wire relift)` FULL equality for several cases including linking/installing? long-and-nested-initializer-transfers uses direct-transfer ✓ full equality. So receive-module rebind is byte-stable. ✓\n\n22. **Now checking the flagged row 17** — the like case. Ruling 2's verbatim harness formulation: \"assert the obligations are names the case's own program defines later (row 20: exactly #{s})\". The engineer counts the `require`'d module's exports as \"names the case's own program defines later\". The work order mentioned \"row 20 and any like case\" per the report. The test asserts excluded ⊂ later store-keys INCLUDING module exports of installing modules. Hmm — the general assertion \"obligations are definitions later in this case\" uses :store-keys of LATER points — installing module exports are answered when the module installs. So host.mod/f IS answered later by construction. The mechanism genuinely verifies \"answered later\". My opinion: consistent with the ruling's general rule; the literal \"defines\" is store-put-oriented, but the ruling's own acceptance sentence says \"lifts only from the first boundary at which its definitions are answered in the store\" — answered is the criterion. Also the orchestrator's work order said \"row 20 and any like case\" — I only have the engineer's word for that, but the report quotes it.\n\n23. **DB concerns: (nth xs 2) try? extraction in frame validate-frames `d`** — for :install xs = [module try?] → second xs = try? ✓; for link xs = [link-id module try? ...] → nth 2 ✓.\n\n24. **`(not (boolean? ...))`** in frame.cljc Q2 — checks try? is boolean ✓. In the row, try? is a :D slot. For link completions lifted via completion-node, (boolean (:try? e)) — always boolean ✓.\n\n25. **Malformed `:install` completion in ready-site-mismatch test**: replaces completion with [:install 'retired false] — site is act at :stream-next? The parked-reader's ready entry state is act with pc after :stream-next... reasons for op :stream-next = #{:next}; :install not in reasons → :reason-mismatch ✓. \n\nNote the halted+ready product: `(assoc completed :halted? true :blocked? false :value 42)` — ready queue nonempty → kind :blocked, result dropped: `(is (nil? (slot t 15)))` ✓ pins dropped result too? Actually this is pinned in ready-site-mismatch-and-halted-product AND halted-with-outstanding-waits-is-normalized-separately ✓ — both acceptance-3 items.\n\n26. Now the remaining piece of rows.cljc I should verify in the worktree: `payload`, `entry`, `state-node` handling, `cell-for!`, `stream-row!`, `analysis` require, plus `lift-task*`'s use of `(:analysis (get-in m [:code segment]))`. And semantic.cljc's ns. Let me read the relevant worktree files.\n\nAlso verify `engine/receive-module` and `module/module-entries`.\n\nAlso check `engine.cljc` for `resume-from-run-queue`, `active-continuation?`, `apply-observation` reason removal.\n\nAlso `unliftable-hold` — check it doesn't include ready-queue refusal.\n\nLet me also verify the doc claim in v2.1 §3.8's sticky check: \"verify that no normative clause still assigns the guard to 6d-b, forbids completed link/install wakes, validates completed installs against live installs, restarts fuel through maybe-run, or promises one-cycle hold resolution.\" The engineer claims it. I could grep the docs in the worktree for these. Let me do targeted greps.\n\nAlso item (d) of my review brief: whether doc amendments contradict retained spec clauses. Noted so far:\n- UCF §7.7.4 \"Quiesce\" step: deliberately left for 6d-b per v2.1 §3.8/6d-b item 8 ✓.\n- v2-amendment version-2 body map: historical ✓.\n\nOne potential contradiction I noticed: yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md kind rules replacement: \"a running activation takes precedence, then a held parked record, then outstanding or completed work, then halt\" ✓ matches. And custody arm table updated to include running ✓.\n\nAnother: `yin.vm.semantic.md` — \"Whole-task quiescence