 receiver's own streams); the stepping source's world is never shared with a receiver; fresh identities normalise through the existing correspondence (`normalize`, `:238`). The reference result and trace are computed once per case on a pristine world.\n332: +\n333: +**Coverage.**\n334: +\n335: +The stepping driver uses returned `run-fuel 1` boundaries. Its coverage classes are body entry, taken and untaken branch, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture, resume, park, FFI park, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, and running install child.\n336: +\n337: +Blocked-plus-ready is tested through an explicit **post-sweep/pre-resume fixture**, constructed with `check-wait-set`. It is not a mandatory returned boundary of the stepping driver. The shared-cursor fixture uses this same seam.\n338: +\n339: +Report driver coverage separately from constructed scheduler-product coverage. Pin per-case/per-host counts after measurement. Neither coverage inventory claims a theorem over all programs.\n340: +\n341: +**Refusal oracle.**\n342: +\n343: +The independent test traversal covers:\n344: +\n345: +- Run windows and lexical bindings.\n346: +- Every K-frame window and lexical binding.\n347: +- Outstanding wait delivery states.\n348: +- Ready delivery states and ready outcome values.\n349: +- Optional ready refusal values and restored value-bearing completion fields.\n350: +- Parked states and recursively reached collections, closures and continuations.\n351: +\n352: +For register targets, exactly the expected unsupported points refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before attachment. Every other point in the admitted backend fragment transfers and continues to the reference outcome. Include a case whose only unsupported primitive is nested in a queued outcome.\n353: +\n354: +Terminal outcomes are compared as outcomes: receiver and source must raise the same classified failure with the same portable diagnostic data after identity normalization, rather than both being required to return a guest value.\n355: +\n356: +**Change:** Consensus P2-7; muse N4/glm O3; glm’s terminal-status acceptance omission.\n357: +\n358: +\n359:  ## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n360:  \n361:  ### 6.1 Code\n362: @@ -1586,7 +1689,7 @@ the shared free environment; refuse, do not convert (R3; §11 6c Q3).\n363:  \n364:  ### 7.1 Lift\n365:  \n366: -**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent; refuse the\n367: +**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): carry ordered completed outcomes; refuse the\n368:  holds (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`) as `:yin.k/non-portable\n369:  :reason-mismatch`; classify kind; run the **dependency closure over the\n370:  `\"v4\"` vectors**, which needs the phase-5 scanners\n371: @@ -1645,19 +1748,17 @@ fetch and admission path, with A the only code identity and the AST\n372:  retained. The holder retains serve answers by `[task-path resource-id]`;\n373:  encode uses those answers without serving externally.\n374:  \n375: +Before entering handles in the external serving table, `lift-task*` runs with temporary plain-data identities to check portability, frames and conservative discharge. This preflight does not call the composition's `serve!` or `:serve-keyed`. A discharge refusal therefore serves zero handles. `discharge!` and its edge-reachability conservatism are unchanged.\n376: +\n377:  The merged order is:\n378:  \n379: -1. **Quiescence and safepoint.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a\n380: -   non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds (`unliftable-hold`: `:observe`,\n381: -   `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`,\n382: -   `:link-cursor-not-installed`) as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`;\n383: -   refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set.\n384: -2. **Kind.** `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked\n385: -   table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted`\n386: -   with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at